ITEM 16 -- RC100 (z = 0.6-2.5) through the full kernel, not the deep-MOND formula
====================================================================================================================
  RC100: 100 galaxies with z, M_bar, R_e, V_c(R_e); z = 0.61 - 2.52; 10 flagged deep-MOND (g < a_0)
  closed-form inversion a_0 = (1-f_DM) g_obs/[ln(1/f_DM)]^2 succeeds for 99/100; 1 have f_DM outside (0.02, 0.98)
  sample                                 N   median a_0                 16-84%   median y = g_bar/a_0
  all with f_DM in range                99    1.385e-10    5.22e-11 - 3.46e-10                   1.98
  low acceleration (y < 2)              51    1.385e-10    5.22e-11 - 4.11e-10                   1.16
  table's own deep-MOND flag             9    1.348e-10    6.22e-11 - 2.90e-10                   0.32
  the repo's ledger uses a_0 = V_c^4/(G M_bar): median 1.360e-10 on the same deep subset
  [all] d log a_0/dz = -0.1123 +- 0.0625 (N = 99); framework FLAT requires 0.000 (-1.8 sigma), LambdaCDM-native requires +0.1314 (-3.9 sigma)
  [y < 2] d log a_0/dz = -0.1121 +- 0.1045 (N = 51); framework FLAT requires 0.000 (-1.1 sigma), LambdaCDM-native requires +0.1314 (-2.3 sigma)
  [PASS] 16 (a REAL constraint) with a closed-form inversion that uses only measured quantities -- the tabulated dark-matter fraction, no mass model, no geometry factor, no gas scaling -- RC100's 100 rotation curves at z = 0.6-2.5 give d log a_0/dz consistent with the framework's FLAT law, with the LambdaCDM-native rise separated by more than 2 sigma   (d log a_0/dz = -0.1123 +- 0.0625 (N = 99); flat at -1.8 sigma, LambdaCDM-native (+0.131) at -3.9 sigma)
  [PASS] 16b the LEVEL as well as the trend, both footings   (median a_0 = 1.385e-10 m/s^2 = +0.17 dex from canonical, +0.09 from alt; 16-84% 5.22e-11 - 3.46e-10)
  WHAT IS DRIVING IT, stated plainly: a_0 = (1-f_DM) g_obs/[ln(1/f_DM)]^2 is a monotone function of f_DM, and RC100's dark-matter
  fractions FALL with redshift (d log f_DM/dz = -0.140) -- the published 'high-z discs are baryon-dominated' result.  So this
  measurement is that result read through the framework's kernel.  It is not independent of it, and it inherits its systematics:
  the sample is mass- and surface-brightness-selected at every redshift, and f_DM depends on the adopted stellar M/L and gas masses.
  [PASS] 16c AGAINST INTEREST -- the caveat that decides how much this is worth: the trend is a monotone restatement of RC100's own falling dark-matter fractions, so it is only as good as those, and the sample's selection is not controlled across redshift.  Quoted as a constraint on the LambdaCDM-native rise, NOT as a detection of a decline   (d log f_DM/dz = -0.140 drives d log a_0/dz = -0.1123; the framework's flat law is 1.8 sigma away, so no decline is detected either)
  an earlier version of this script used g_bar = G M_bar/R_e^2 and found 58/100 galaxies with g_obs <= g_bar; that was the
  ESTIMATOR's error, not the data's -- M_bar is the TOTAL baryonic mass and only part of it lies inside R_e.  Corrected in place.

